  "notes": "The Sony Nashville A&R-producer who signed and built three very different acts, so \"his vocabulary tracks the act\" — which is why this model carries both the documented four-chord/sus lane and the southern-rock mixolydian one as families rather than picking one. One progression and one range are documented outright: \"C major, C–F/C–C–Gsus4 (I – IV/1 – I – Vsus4), vocal range G3–C5,\" which is MIDI 55–72 and is the melody register with no inference. His signature is a stated inversion of the usual dynamic lever: \"mixed drier and smaller than his contemporaries — his signature is that **arrangement, not kit size, does the dynamic work**: 'drums, fiddle, and soft guitar and piano' in verses opening to a banjo line at the chorus. Cross-stick and shaker verses; brushes on acoustic cuts.\" He came up as a rock sound engineer, \"which explains the roomier drum treatment on the Montgomery Gentry records,\" so the jitter is mid rather than tight. The bass is \"sparse and low in the mix, leaving the mid-low to banjo and dobro; upright on acoustic material,\" hence a low register ceiling. One production practice is worth its own note and is authored as a high counter density: \"the producers themselves singing background vocals … background vocals are a production layer, not a session hire.\" ⚠ Confidence is medium (research: medium-high). ⚠ The INFERRED parameters are the velocity tiers, the perc density and the counter ratio.",
